<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio de Nebrija</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1444">1444</a>–<a href="/years/1522">1522</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Elio Antonio de Nebrija</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio de Nebrija was a Castilian humanist who taught at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> and Alcalá. His Gramática de la lengua castellana of 1492, presented to Isabella I, was the first printed grammar of a modern European vernacular. He also wrote a widely used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> grammar and dictionaries and worked on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complutensian-polyglot-bible">Complutensian Polyglot Bible</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-de-nebrija.jpg" alt="Antonio de Nebrija" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reproducción del grabado de Brieva publicado por la Calcografía nacional. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_de_Nebrija_(1892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nebrija was baptised Antonio Martínez de Cala and Latinized his name as Aelius Antonius Nebrissensis, taking Aelius from the Roman inscriptions of his native Lebrija, called Nebrissa Veneria in Roman times. He was born into an hidalgo family in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and 1444 is the traditionally accepted year. At fourteen he enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a>. A scholarship from the bishopric of Seville took him to the Royal College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, where he was inspired by Italian humanists, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a>. After ten years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> he returned to Spain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served the archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, Alonso de Fonseca y Ulloa, for three years, and after Fonseca died in 1473 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> as a lecturer. In 1476 he was appointed to the First Chair of Grammar, and in 1481 he published the Introductiones latinae, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> textbook whose first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> of 1,000 copies sold out and which was reprinted dozens of times. After his marriage in 1487 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> under the patronage of Juan de Zúñiga, master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-alcantara">Order of Alcántara</a>, and lived there for twelve years.</p><p>In 1492 he published the Gramática de la lengua castellana, dedicated to Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a>, one of the first books to codify a European vernacular. In the dedication he called language the instrument of empire and suggested the grammar would serve when the Catholic monarchs ruled peoples speaking other languages. In 1492 he also published a Latin-Spanish dictionary and about 1495 reversed it into the Vocabulario español-latino, which became the basis for later dictionaries, including Spanish-Arabic (1505), Spanish-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> (1547) and Spanish-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> (1613).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Nebrija then turned to biblical scholarship, applying the critical methods Italian humanists used on classical texts. Around 1504 Diego de Deza, the Grand Inquisitor, confiscated and destroyed his work, but Cardinal Jiménez de Cisneros, who succeeded Deza in 1507, let him resume. He briefly served on the committee preparing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/complutensian-polyglot-bible">Complutensian Polyglot Bible</a> and clashed with its conservative editors, and his input was largely ignored when it appeared in 1517. He also wrote on theology, law, archaeology, pedagogy and rhetoric. He died on 5 July 1522 in Alcalá de Henares.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonio de Nebrija” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Antonio de Nebrija</b>'s Gramática de la lengua castellana of 1492 was the first printed grammar of a modern European vernacular.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Elio Antonio de Nebrija</b> dated the introduction to his Gramática de la lengua castellana, written in Salamanca and the first grammar of a modern European language, writing that language was always the companion of empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lebrija, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Alcalá de Henares, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.92,-6.08&amp;year=1494" aria-label="Lebrija on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Antonio de Nebrija"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4585 4399h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4585 4399h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.92,-6.08&amp;year=1494">What was here in 1494? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
